{
  "slug": "etre-avoir",
  "number": 4,
  "part": 1,
  "title": "Être and avoir",
  "tagline": "The two most used verbs: conjugation, uses and where French says «have» but English says «be».",
  "goal": "Conjugate être (to be) and avoir (to have) without thinking, tell ils sont from ils ont by ear and avoid the classic traps like «I am hungry» or «I am 40».",
  "minutes": 10,
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "importancia",
      "title": "Why they matter so much",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "*Être* and *avoir* are the most frequent verbs in French. You use them to introduce yourself, describe things, talk about age and how you feel, and they are also the **auxiliaries** of the past tense [[10]], just like «have» in «I have eaten». Both are irregular: they have to be memorised."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "etre",
      "title": "Être: to be",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "conjugation",
          "persons": [
            "je",
            "tu",
            "il / elle / on",
            "nous",
            "vous",
            "ils / elles"
          ],
          "verbs": [
            {
              "verb": "être",
              "meaning": "to be",
              "forms": [
                "je {suis}",
                "tu {es}",
                "il {est}",
                "nous {sommes}",
                "vous {êtes}",
                "ils {sont}"
              ],
              "notes": [
                "«swee»",
                "«ay»",
                "«ay»",
                "«som»",
                "«voo-zet», with the liaison",
                "«sohn», with a nasal o"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "title": "On",
          "text": "*on* means «one», «we» or «people» and takes the same form as *il*. In conversation *on* is the usual way to say «we»: *On est prêts* = We're ready."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "**Uses:** nationality (*Je suis canadienne*), job (*Il est médecin*: no article, unlike «he's a doctor»), description (*Elle est grande*), state (*Nous sommes contents*) and location (*Vous êtes à Paris*)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "avoir",
      "title": "Avoir: to have",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "conjugation",
          "persons": [
            "je",
            "tu",
            "il / elle / on",
            "nous",
            "vous",
            "ils / elles"
          ],
          "verbs": [
            {
              "verb": "avoir",
              "meaning": "to have",
              "forms": [
                "j'{ai}",
                "tu {as}",
                "il {a}",
                "nous {avons}",
                "vous {avez}",
                "ils {ont}"
              ],
              "notes": [
                "«zhay»",
                "«ah»",
                "«ah»",
                "«noo-za-vohn»",
                "«voo-za-vay»",
                "«eel-zohn»"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "title": "The French j",
          "text": "The *j* in *j'ai* is not the English «j» of «jam»: it's the soft sound in «measure» or «vision»."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "text": "*je* + *ai* = *j'ai*. Because *ai* starts with a vowel, *je* loses its e. Never «je ai»."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "trampas",
      "title": "When English says «be» and French says «have»",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "caption": "Expressions with avoir",
          "head": [
            "French",
            "English"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*avoir faim*",
              "to be hungry"
            ],
            [
              "*avoir soif*",
              "to be thirsty"
            ],
            [
              "*avoir chaud / froid*",
              "to be hot / cold"
            ],
            [
              "*avoir peur*",
              "to be afraid"
            ],
            [
              "*avoir sommeil*",
              "to be sleepy"
            ],
            [
              "*avoir raison / tort*",
              "to be right / wrong"
            ],
            [
              "*avoir … ans*",
              "to be … years old"
            ],
            [
              "*avoir besoin de*",
              "to need"
            ],
            [
              "*avoir envie de*",
              "to feel like"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "The two classic mistakes",
          "text": "«I'm 40» = *J'ai quarante ans*, never «je suis quarante». «I'm cold» = *J'ai froid*; *je suis froid* means «I'm a cold person»."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sont-ont",
      "title": "Ils sont or ils ont",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "head": [
            "",
            "Pronounced",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "*ils {s}ont*",
              "«eel-sohn», with a hissing s",
              "they are"
            ],
            [
              "*ils {ont}*",
              "«eel-zohn», with a buzzing z",
              "they have"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "tip",
          "text": "If you hear a buzz [z], it's *avoir*. If you hear a hiss [s], it's *être*. That [z] is a *liaison* [[14]]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "negacion",
      "title": "In the negative",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "ne + verb + pas",
          "caption": "*Je {ne} suis {pas} fatigué.*"
        },
        {
          "type": "examples",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "Tu n'es pas seul.",
              "tr": "You're not alone.",
              "note": "ne → n' before a vowel"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Je n'ai pas de voiture.",
              "tr": "I don't have a car.",
              "note": "un → de in the negative [[6]]"
            },
            {
              "fr": "Nous n'avons pas faim.",
              "tr": "We're not hungry."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "There's more on negation in [[12]]."
        }
      ]
    }
